Make select5 exit non-zero when it stops on an error
====================================================

Applies to:   $VALD_HOME/SOURCE/SELECT/select5.f  (VALD3 SVN, not this repo)
Generated:    2026-09-08
Status:       NOT APPLIED - compile-checked only, never installed

Why
---
Every STOP in select5.f is bare, and a bare STOP exits 0. So select5 reports a
fatal error on stdout and then tells its caller it succeeded. The pipeline in
vald/job_runner.py checks return codes, sees zero, and reports whatever it can
infer from the missing output file.

That is how the 2026-09 model-grid rename surfaced: five extractstellar
requests failed with

    Output file not found: Polina.757221

while the actual cause, printed to stdout and discarded, was

    SELECT3: Wrong model atmosphere fielname:/home/vald/VALD3/MODELS/STELLAR/07900G03.KRZ

Two independent changes came out of that. The app side is done and committed -
select5's stdout is now captured to <job>/select5.out and used in the error
message, and _find_model no longer invents a filename. This patch is the other
half: making the exit status tell the truth, so a failure is caught by the
return-code check rather than inferred from a missing file.

What it changes
---------------
20 of the 22 bare STOPs become STOP 1. Under gfortran that sets exit status 1
and prints "STOP 1" on stderr; summarise_stage_error() already filters that
line, so it will not displace the real message.

The two deliberately left alone are normal terminations, not errors:

  line 142  end of the usage text, printed when the control file asks for help
  line 763  label 5, reached by END= on the control-file read. The comment
            above it reads "END-OF-FILE MEANS THE END OF TABLES COMPUTING" -
            running out of input is how a multi-region run finishes, so this
            must keep exiting 0 or every successful stellar job starts failing.

Nothing else is touched: no message text, no logic, no formats. STOP with an
integer literal is FORTRAN 77, so it needs no dialect flag.

Applying it
-----------
    cd $VALD_HOME
    patch -p1 --dry-run < /path/to/select5-stop-codes.patch   # verify first
    patch -p1 < /path/to/select5-stop-codes.patch
    cd SOURCE/SELECT && make select5

Then install by hand, as with the showline fix - the Makefile leaves the binary
in SOURCE/SELECT:

    cp SOURCE/SELECT/select5 $VALD_HOME/bin/select5

Verifying
---------
A missing model atmosphere should now be a non-zero exit:

    printf "5000.,5010.,0.05,2.0\n'/nonexistent.krz'\n'END'\n'Synth'\n'select.out'\n500000\n" > select.input
    preselect5 < pres_in.NNNNNN | select5 ; echo "select5 rc=${PIPESTATUS[1]}"
    # before: rc=0, no select.out, nothing in the app's error message
    # after:  rc=1, and the app reports "select5 failed: SELECT3: Wrong model
    #         atmosphere fielname:..."

A normal run must still exit 0, and a multi-region control file must still
terminate cleanly on EOF - that is the regression this patch could plausibly
introduce, so test it before installing.

Compile-checked with the flags the tree actually uses - CONFIG/Makefile_local.inc
sets F77FLAG = -O2, with -std=legacy commented out since presformat5.f's
missing-comma FORMATs were fixed:

    cd $VALD_HOME/SOURCE/SELECT
    gfortran -c -O2 -I../PRESELECT select5.f    # exit 0, and compiler output
                                                # byte-identical to unpatched

Note that gfortran 16 needs the EXTERNAL split fix for a full clean build of
the tree; that is unrelated to this patch.

